    "body": "**TLDR:** Math-Verify reads LaTeX only inside a delimiter like `\\boxed{}`, and our gold answers are bare. So gold `2\\sqrt{3}` was read as 2: the model's `\\boxed{2}` scored 1.0 and the right answer 0.0. Now the gold is boxed like the prediction. No change on DAPO-Math (integer golds); ~19% of INTELLECT-3-RL math golds were misread.\n\n**Results**\n```text\nmath-verify 0.9.0, score_math_response(response, gold)   before -> after\ngold 2\\sqrt{3}, response \\boxed{2\\sqrt{3}}               0.0 -> 1.0\ngold 2\\sqrt{3}, response \\boxed{2}                       1.0 -> 0.0\ngold (1,2),     response \\boxed{(1,2)}                   0.0 -> 1.0\ngold \\pi/4,     response \\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}           0.0 -> 1.0\n```\nToday's recipes score the same: every DAPO-Math-17k (12,643) and AIME 2025 (30) gold parses to the same value either way. On 10,805 INTELLECT-3-RL math rows, 949 golds parse to nothing bare and 1,126 to a different value (`3:5` as 3). Counted as `parse(gold)` vs `parse(\"\\\\boxed{\" + gold + \"}\")`.\n\nNOTE: an integer gold with a trailing period (`34.`) now reads as the string `34.`, so `\\boxed{34}` goes 1.0 -> 0.0. No DAPO-Math-17k or AIME 2025 gold has one.\n\nNOTE: the grader-process PR moves `score_math_response` to `grader.py`; whichever lands second rebases this one line.\n\nYou may ask:\n- Is this standard? verl (`math_verify.py:59`), NeMo-RL (`math_environment.py:134`) and verifiers (`score.py:103`) box the gold the same way.\n- Why not a Math-Verify extraction config? Its LaTeX patterns need a delimiter (`$`, `\\(`, `\\[`, `\\boxed`), so bare LaTeX falls to the number parser, which reads `2\\sqrt{3}` as 2.\n\n**Test plan**\n`pytest tests/rl/unit_tests/cpu/test_dapo_math.py`: 11 passed; the new test fails with the base rubric.\n\ncc @pzhan9\n",
